/** \page utilities Utilities

@section utilities_StringConv String Conversions

A set of functions offers fast conversions between strings and numbers. The
overloaded functions Pt::parseInt() and Pt::formatInt() convert between strings
and integers, and Pt::parseFloat() and Pt::formatFloat() convert between
strings and floats. The functions work with iterators as input or output
instead of string objects, so they can work even with simple buffers or streams
using the stream iterators from the C++ standard library, as shown in the 
following example:

@code
#include <Pt/Convert.h>
#incldue <iterator>
#inlcude <iostream>

std::ostream_iterator<char> it(std::cout);
Pt::formatInt(it, 42);

const char* buf = "42";
const char* bufend = buf + 2;
int n = 0;
Pt::parseInt(buf, bufend, n);
@endcode

A format object can be passed as an additional argument to specify the aspects
of localisation, for example, how characters are converted or which characters
are used for the plus and minus signs. The overloads, which do not use a format
object, use decimal format. A few format objects are already provided by the
%Pt library, named Pt::DecimalFormat, Pt::OctalFormat, Pt::HexFormat or 
Pt::BinaryFormat. They work for ASCII compatible encodings like Latin-1 or the
unicode encodings. Besides localization, they also define the base for the
numeric conversion. The next example shows how integers in hex format can be
parsed and formatted:

@code
#include <Pt/Convert.h>
#incldue <iterator>
#include <iostream>

Pt::HexFormat<char> fmt;

std::ostream_iterator<char> it(std::cout);
Pt::formatInt(it, 42, fmt);

const char* buf = "42";
const char* bufend = buf + 2;
int n = 0;
Pt::parseInt(buf, bufend, n, fmt);
@endcode

Floating point numbers can be formatted and parsed in a similar way like
integers, but the format object needs to provide more information than the
format object used for integers. The library provides Pt::FloatFormat, which
is also used for the overloads that do not take a format object as an
argument. For formatting, the precision can additionally be set:

@code
#include <Pt/Convert.h>
#incldue <iterator>
#inlcude <iostream>

Pt::FloatFormat<char> fmt;

std::ostream_iterator<char> it(std::cout);
int precision = 5;
Pt::formatFloat(it, 42.123, fmt, precision);

const char* buf = "42.123";
const char* bufend = buf + 6;
int f = 0;
Pt::parseFloat(buf, bufend, f);
@endcode

All parse functions used so far throw an exception of type Pt::ConversionError,
if the conversion failed. Overloads of the parse functions are available, which
set a bool flag instead, to indicate a conversion error. Both, the format and
parse functions return an iterator pointing to the position after the last
character that was written or read, respectively. Partial consumption of the
input is not treated as an error.

@section utilities_NumConv Numeric Conversions

Numeric assignments can lead to loss of data, if the operation narrows the
data type to a smaller one. For example, numeric conversion from int to short
can be an error, if the assigned value exceeds the maximum or minimum value
possible for shorts. The narrow() function can be used instead of normal
assignment, to protect against this. In case of an error, an exception
of type Pt::ConversionError is thrown.

@code
#include <Pt/Convert.h>
#include <limits>
#include <iostream>

long l = ...;
short s = 0;

try
{
    s = Pt::narrow<short>(l);
}
catch(const Pt::ConversionError& e)
{
    std::cerr << "numeric value is out of range: " << l << std::endl;
}
@endcode

@section utilities_ByteOrder Byte Order Conversion

The byteorder conversion API consists of two sets of functions. The Pt::swab()
function is able to swap the byteorder of a type by bytewise copying as shown
in the following example:

@code
#include <Pt/Byteorder.h>

Pt::uint32_t value = ...;
Pt::uint32_t swapped = Pt::swab(value);
@endcode

The swab() function is overloaded for all fixed-size integer types such as
Pt::uint32_t. A second set of functions can be used to convert from a specific
external byteorder to the native host byteorder:

- Pt::beToHost()
- Pt::hostToBe()
- Pt::leToHost()
- Pt::hostToLe()

For example the function beToHost() converts from big-endian to the host byte
order:

@code
#include <Pt/Byteorder.h>

Pt::uint32_t beVal = ...;
Pt::uint32_t value = Pt::beToHost(beVal);
@endcode

@section utilities_Any The Any Class

